      <p><strong>What is the tiny house movement?</strong> It is a loose, grassroots shift toward living in much smaller homes, often under 400 sq ft, to cut housing costs, avoid large mortgages, own fewer possessions and live more intentionally. It grew out of simple-living ideas, then accelerated after the 2008 housing crisis made big, debt-financed houses look risky.</p>
      <p>There is no central organization, membership or official definition behind it. The movement is really a set of shared values, expressed through tiny houses on wheels, backyard cottages, small cabins, converted sheds and containers. This guide explains where it started, why it caught on, and what it means for anyone thinking about joining it.</p>

