      <h2 id="definition">What is tiny house movement, in plain terms?</h2>
      <p>People phrase the question differently, but the answer is the same: the tiny house movement is the choice to deliberately live with less space than the typical American home provides, in exchange for lower costs and more freedom. A few ideas run through almost every version of it:</p>
      <ul class="check-list">
        <li><strong>Right-sized space.</strong> Build only the square footage you actually use, rather than paying to heat, clean and finance rooms that sit empty.</li>
        <li><strong>Less debt.</strong> A smaller home can often be paid off in a few years or bought outright, instead of carrying a 30-year mortgage.</li>
        <li><strong>Fewer possessions.</strong> Living small forces you to keep what matters and let go of the rest.</li>
        <li><strong>A smaller footprint.</strong> Less material to build, less energy to run.</li>
        <li><strong>Self-reliance.</strong> Many tiny homeowners design or build their own homes, and some go off-grid.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>What counts as "tiny" is debated. The model building code's tiny house appendix covers homes of 400 sq ft or less, and that number is a common reference point. For a fuller definition and the main types, see <a href="/blog/what-is-a-tiny-house/">what is a tiny house?</a> and <a href="/blog/how-big-is-a-tiny-home/">how big is a tiny home?</a></p>

      <h2 id="history">Where the tiny house movement started</h2>
      <p>Small dwellings are as old as housing itself. Cabins, cottages, bungalows and camps were ordinary homes for generations, and the idea of a simple life in a small house has deep roots in American culture, from Henry David Thoreau's one-room cabin at Walden Pond to the backyard cottages built in many early twentieth-century neighborhoods. What changed was the direction of the mainstream: over the second half of the twentieth century, the average new American home grew steadily larger while households got smaller.</p>
      <p>The modern tiny house movement formed as a reaction to that trend. In the late 1990s and early 2000s, architects, writers and small-home designers began arguing for houses built around quality and fit rather than size, and the broader simple-living and voluntary-simplicity ideas of the period gave those arguments an audience. A handful of designers started building very small homes on trailers, partly because a house on wheels could sidestep the minimum dwelling sizes written into many zoning codes. That combination, a small, well-crafted home on a trailer, became the defining image of the movement.</p>

