      <h2 id="why">Why the tiny house movement grew</h2>
      <h3>The 2008 housing crisis</h3>
      <p>The collapse of the housing market around 2008 is the turning point most people point to. Millions of households saw foreclosures, falling home values or tighter credit up close, and the idea that a bigger house was always a safe investment took a hit. A home you could pay for in cash, or pay off in a few years, suddenly looked less like a quirk and more like a hedge.</p>
      <h3>Rising housing costs and debt</h3>
      <p>In the years since, high home prices, high rents and student debt have kept the financial argument alive, especially for younger buyers priced out of their local markets and retirees looking to downsize without draining savings. Our guide to <a href="/blog/how-much-does-a-tiny-house-cost/">how much a tiny house costs</a> breaks down what building one really involves.</p>
      <h3>Media and the internet</h3>
      <p>In the 2010s, cable TV shows about building, buying and living in tiny houses brought the idea into living rooms across the country, while blogs, YouTube channels and Instagram accounts gave people a close look at daily life in 200 sq ft. Plans, build videos and online communities made it far easier for an ordinary person to try building one.</p>
      <h3>Environmental and lifestyle goals</h3>
      <p>For many, the appeal was never only financial. A smaller home uses less material and energy, and living with less appeals to people tired of maintaining a large house full of things. The broader benefits are covered in our guide to <a href="/embracing-tiny-homes-unlocking-the-advantages-of-compact-living/">the advantages of compact living</a>. In the 2020s, the spread of remote work added another motivation for some: if your job can go anywhere, a smaller or mobile home becomes more practical.</p>

      <h2 id="who">Who lives tiny today</h2>
      <p>The stereotype is a young couple on a trailer in the woods, but the people drawn to the tiny house movement are far more varied.</p>
