      <h2 id="impact">What the tiny house movement changed</h2>
      <p>For years, the biggest obstacle for tiny homeowners was simply being legal. Many zoning codes set minimum house sizes, and the building code had no provisions for very small homes or sleeping lofts. Pressure from the movement helped change that. The 2018 International Residential Code added an appendix specifically for tiny houses of 400 sq ft or less (now Appendix AQ), covering ceiling heights, lofts and loft access. Many states and cities have also loosened rules for accessory dwelling units, and RV and park model certification gave builders of tiny homes on wheels a recognized standard to build to.</p>
      <p>Adoption is uneven, and rules vary widely from one county to the next. Our <a href="/blog/tiny-house-zoning-laws/">tiny house zoning laws guide</a> explains how to check what applies where you want to live, and <a href="/blog/are-tiny-homes-considered-mobile-homes/">are tiny homes considered mobile homes?</a> covers how they are classified.</p>

      <div class="callout wood">
        <strong>The legal question comes first</strong>
        <p>The movement's enthusiasm sometimes outruns the rules. Before buying land or a trailer, confirm with your local planning office that a tiny home is allowed where you plan to put it. Regulations change often and differ by jurisdiction.</p>
      </div>

      <h2 id="realities">Criticisms and realities</h2>
      <p>An honest picture of the tiny house movement includes its downsides. Tiny homes on wheels are often hard to finance and insure, and they usually lose value over time like RVs rather than gaining it like houses. Finding a legal, affordable place to park can be harder than building the house. Per square foot, tiny homes cost more to build than large ones, because kitchens, bathrooms and systems are packed into a small space. And some people discover that a 200 sq ft home is too small for their household, especially once children or a home office enter the picture. If you are weighing the money side, read <a href="/blog/are-tiny-homes-worth-it/">are tiny homes worth it?</a></p>

      <h2 id="today">The tiny house movement today</h2>
      <p>The movement has matured. The early image of a DIY house on a trailer still exists, but much of the energy has shifted toward backyard ADUs, small cottages on foundations, tiny home communities and professionally built, certified homes. That shift tracks the legal landscape: foundation homes and ADUs are often easier to permit, finance and resell. At Tiny Homes Blueprint, we see the same thing in the plans people ask for, from compact trailer homes to one- and two-bedroom cottages.</p>

      <h2 id="join">How to join the tiny house movement</h2>
      <p>If the idea appeals to you, start with practical questions rather than aesthetics:</p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Find a legal location.</strong> Read <a href="/blog/where-to-put-a-tiny-house/">where to put a tiny house</a> and check your county's rules.</li>
        <li><strong>Size it honestly.</strong> The <a href="/tools/how-big-should-my-tiny-house-be/">tiny house size calculator</a> estimates the square footage your household needs.</li>
        <li><strong>Choose wheels or a foundation.</strong> Mobility costs you space, weight capacity and often legal certainty.</li>
        <li><strong>Budget the whole project.</strong> Land, utilities and permits can rival the cost of the house. Try the <a href="/tools/tiny-house-cost-calculator/">tiny house cost calculator</a>.</li>
        <li><strong>Pick a plan and build path.</strong> Buy a finished home, hire a builder or build it yourself using <a href="/blog/how-to-build-a-tiny-house/">our step-by-step build guide</a>.</li>
      </ol>
